WORKED EXAMPLE — what 'assert the property, not the spelling' actually looks like, from #205.

Its bulk-paths.AC-66-69 test was red because it asserted the literal string 'recipientsFor' in the source of sendComms and MRQ-180's refactor renamed that call to resolveReminderSelection. I asked its implementer for one of two fixes: assert the property, or widen the pattern and say why the name is load-bearing. It took a third option that is better than both.

It replaced the regex with a TypeScript AST walk — ts.createSourceFile over the module, then structural assertions:

  assert.ok(sendComms, 'sendComms must remain discoverable as the communication write seam');
  assert.ok(selectionInitializer, 'sendComms must retain a selection result before enqueueing');
  assert.ok(ts.isAwaitExpression(selectionInitializer), 'sendComms must await its canonical selection path');
  assert.ok(ts.isCallExpression(selectionInitializer.expression), 'sendComms must call the selection path');

It now pins the SHAPE — an awaited call expression whose result feeds the enqueue — rather than the identifier. It survives a rename by construction, which is exactly what the string was failing to do, and it still catches the thing the original test existed to catch: someone bypassing the shared selection seam.

Use this as the pattern for the other thirty. 'Assert the property, not the spelling' is abstract advice; this is what it looks like when someone does it, and the ts AST is already a dependency here. Note also what it did NOT do: it did not delete the test, and it did not move the guarantee somewhere weaker.
